        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'The most abused adjustment in non-GAAP reporting is the "restructuring charge." By definition, restructuring is non-recurring — you are reorganizing the company once to improve its structure. But some companies take restructuring charges every single year, each time labeled as a one-time event. Over a decade, a company might exclude $5–10 billion in cumulative restructuring charges from non-GAAP earnings while GAAP earnings look dramatically worse. The recurring restructuring charge is not a non-GAAP adjustment — it is a recurring operating cost that management has chosen to rebrand as exceptional.'
