        text: 'Two components of EV that often confuse analysts are minority interest and preferred stock. Minority interest (also called non-controlling interest) appears on the balance sheet when a company consolidates a subsidiary it does not fully own. For example, if Company A owns 80% of Subsidiary B, it consolidates 100% of Subsidiary B\'s revenue and assets, but a 20% minority interest appears on the consolidated balance sheet. When calculating EV, you add back this minority interest because an acquirer of Company A would need to also acquire the remaining 20% of Subsidiary B. Preferred stock is added for similar reasons — preferred shareholders have a senior claim that an acquirer must satisfy.'
