        type: 'example',
        company: 'A Hypothetical Retailer',
        scenario: 'Imagine a retailer with $500M in revenue, $50M in net income, and $80M in receivables growth (customers are paying more slowly). Under the indirect method, you see: Net Income $50M, add D&A $30M, subtract receivables increase ($80M) = Operating Cash Flow $0M. Under the direct method, you\'d see: Cash collected from customers = $420M (not $500M — $80M is still owed). The direct method makes it immediately obvious that the company collected far less cash than it booked as revenue. The indirect method buries this in a working capital line that many investors skip.',
        numbers: {
          'Revenue': '$500M',
          'Net Income': '$50M',
          'D&A': '$30M',
          'Receivables Increase': '$80M',
          'Operating Cash Flow (both methods)': '$0M',
          'Cash Collected from Customers (direct)': '$420M'
        }
