        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'Academic research consistently documents that stocks with the most pessimistic consensus estimates generate superior subsequent returns. The De Bondt and Thaler (1985) study showed that the 35 worst-performing stocks over 3-5 years outperformed the 35 best performers in the subsequent period by approximately 25 percentage points. This mean reversion effect is driven by representativeness bias — investors anchor too strongly to recent performance and extrapolate trends that are already priced into (or out of) the stock.',
