        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'The Sharpe ratio is the most widely used risk-adjusted return metric in finance. But it has a fundamental flaw: it treats all volatility as bad. A fund that frequently delivers large positive surprises gets penalised exactly as much as one that delivers large negative surprises — even though investors welcome positive surprises. The Sortino ratio corrects this by measuring only downside volatility.'
