        text: 'The trailing P/E uses the actual earnings from the last twelve months — it is backward-looking. The forward P/E uses analyst estimates of earnings over the next twelve months — it is forward-looking. Both are useful. Trailing P/E is based on real reported numbers; forward P/E reflects expectations. A stock with a high trailing P/E but a much lower forward P/E (because earnings are expected to grow sharply) may actually be fairly valued or cheap when growth is considered.',
