        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'Only large institutions called Authorized Participants (APs) — typically major broker-dealers — can create or redeem ETF shares directly with the fund. Creation: when an ETF trades at a premium to its Net Asset Value (NAV), an AP buys the underlying basket of securities in the open market, delivers them to the ETF issuer, and receives newly created ETF shares to sell on the exchange — a risk-free arbitrage that supplies more shares and pushes the ETF price back down toward NAV. Redemption is the reverse: when the ETF trades at a discount, the AP buys ETF shares cheaply, redeems them with the issuer for the underlying basket, and sells the basket at a profit.',
