        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'For a portfolio of N equally weighted, equally volatile assets with uniform pairwise correlation ρ, portfolio variance equals σ²[ρ + (1-ρ)/N]. As N grows large, the (1-ρ)/N term disappears and portfolio variance converges to σ²ρ — the irreducible systematic risk. This means diversification eliminates all idiosyncratic (company-specific) risk but cannot eliminate market-wide risk. With ρ = 0.3 (typical within a single equity market), roughly 20–30 stocks eliminates most diversifiable risk.'
