        text: 'The disposition effect was named by Hersh Shefrin and Meir Statman in 1985, drawing on Kahneman and Tversky\'s Prospect Theory. The core observation: investors are "disposed" to sell winners and hold losers. The pattern appears among retail investors, institutional investors, and even professional fund managers. It is one of the most robust findings in behavioral finance — and one of the most expensive. Investors who exhibit the disposition effect effectively prune their best ideas and water their worst ones.',
