        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'In a total return swap (TRS), one party pays the total return of a reference asset (dividends plus capital appreciation) and receives a floating rate plus a spread. The payer gives up the upside (and covers the downside) of the asset; the receiver gets all economic exposure without owning the asset. Hedge funds use TRS extensively to gain leveraged exposure to equities, bonds, or credit indexes without using direct capital. Archegos Capital Management famously accumulated massive hidden exposure through TRS with multiple prime brokers, which led to its catastrophic collapse in 2021 when banks simultaneously unwound positions.',
