          'Loss aversion: losses feel approximately twice as painful as equivalent gains feel pleasurable (Kahneman and Tversky). Traders hold losers too long (hoping for a recovery to avoid realizing the pain) and sell winners too quickly (locking in the pleasure of a gain). This systematically produces the worst possible outcome.',
          'Overconfidence: most traders believe they are above average. After a string of wins, they increase position sizes beyond what their edge justifies, then suffer a catastrophic loss.',
          'Recency bias: overweighting recent events in forming expectations. After a bull run, traders expect more bullishness; after a crash, they expect further crashes. Both lead to poor timing.',
          'Anchoring: fixating on an arbitrary reference price (the price you bought at, a 52-week high) when making decisions, rather than assessing current value independently.',
          'Confirmation bias: seeking information that confirms existing positions and ignoring information that contradicts them. Fatal in earnings seasons when new data should update views.',
          'Revenge trading: after a loss, immediately taking a larger, impulsive trade to "win it back." This is emotional decision-making at its most dangerous and accounts for many of the largest single-day losses traders experience.',
