        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'One of the most important but least-discussed questions in backtesting is: how much historical data do you need before a result is statistically meaningful? The answer depends on the Sharpe ratio of the strategy, the desired level of statistical confidence, and the number of independent observations in your sample. For most realistic strategies, the required data is far more than most investors appreciate — often 10–20 years of monthly data minimum, and much more for strategies with lower Sharpe ratios or higher noise.',
