        text: 'Tail-risk hedging means paying a premium today to limit catastrophic losses in a severe market event. Like fire insurance, the protection costs money every year and feels wasteful in good times — but its value in a genuine crisis can be enormous. The central challenge is that tail hedges are expensive precisely because tail events are rare; implied volatility and option premiums embed a fear premium that means most tail hedges expire worthless. The art is in sizing and structuring hedges so they pay off when you need them without bleeding the portfolio in normal markets.',
