          'Uncovered Interest Rate Parity (UIP) theory says carry trades should not work: if Country A has 5% interest rates and Country B has 0%, UIP predicts Country A\'s currency should depreciate by 5% annually — exactly offsetting the carry. In practice, UIP fails empirically: high-yield currencies do not depreciate as fast as predicted, leaving a persistent net carry profit.',
          'Risk characteristics: Currency carry trades have positive expected return most of the time but suffer from crash risk — during "risk-off" episodes (crises, recessions), investors liquidate carry positions simultaneously, causing the funding currency (yen, franc) to appreciate sharply and the target currencies to fall violently. The 2008 yen carry unwind saw the yen appreciate 30% against the Australian dollar in weeks.',
