        type: 'paragraph',
        text: 'Portfolio turnover — the percentage of portfolio holdings replaced in a given year — is one of the most directly controllable determinants of a systematic strategy\'s real-world returns. High turnover keeps factor exposures fresh but generates significant transaction costs and tax drag that consume alpha. Low turnover reduces costs but allows factor exposures to drift as positions age out of the target range. Finding the optimal turnover for a given strategy requires explicitly modeling both the signal half-life (how quickly the factor signal decays) and the total cost of trading.',
